00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:29.960
- So in closing just one
more question from Facebook,

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:32.480
Josh Carroll asks, are there ways

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:34.470
to perhaps help parents teach math

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:36.690
in ways that show the applied side,

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:39.670
say show how to apply quadratic functions

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:41.320
to things like parabolic machine?

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:46.600
- Yeah I mean for that particular example,

00:27:46.600 --> 00:27:48.170
if kids are curious you could actually

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:49.830
start going into the physics

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:51.310
which we have on Khan Academy

00:27:51.310 --> 00:27:53.370
and if the students
already know quadratics

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:55.450
and actually if they even know just

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:57.100
the basics of trigonometry which hopefully

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:58.820
they got from some of
their geometry classes.

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:00.540
But you could even go over that with them.

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:03.300
They're ready to go into
things like projectile motion,

00:28:03.300 --> 00:28:04.760
two-dimensional projectile motion.

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:07.573
And you know one-dimensional
projectile motion,

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.060
you actually don't need any trigonometry

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:11.607
you just need to know a
little bit about quadratics

00:28:11.607 --> 00:28:13.860
and so that's a very
interesting application.

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:16.100
You can actually see how
good your predictions hold up

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:19.400
or you can use the math to
understand and go outside,

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:21.410
use a timer throw a ball in the air,

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:22.580
figure out how long it's in the air

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:24.210
and then based on that you can figure out

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:26.250
how high it went, which is kind of cool.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:27.950
You can figure out how high a ball went

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:29.380
by timing it and then you could say

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:30.470
well how exact is it,

00:28:30.470 --> 00:28:32.780
because in our equations we
didn't have air resistance,

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:34.180
now we do and things like that.

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:36.830
But I think that's actually a lot of fun.

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:38.910
And then obviously you
can do the same thing

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:39.743
with two dimensions.

00:28:39.743 --> 00:28:42.170
You can figure out the angle
by maybe where it lands

00:28:42.170 --> 00:28:44.360
or how far it went based
on measuring you know

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:45.610
there's all sorts of interesting things

00:28:45.610 --> 00:28:47.131
you could do with that.

00:28:47.131 --> 00:28:50.650
So physics is a great place
to apply that type of thing.

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:53.800
You'll see a lot of that
in economics as well,

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:54.990
and a lot of the basic algebra

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:58.760
actually you'll see in
in chemistry as well

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:01.180
and actually some of the
genetic stuff in biology.

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:03.120
So I think those are
all really interesting.

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:05.183
They obviously exponential growth.

00:29:05.183 --> 00:29:06.603
(laughing)

00:29:06.603 --> 00:29:07.436
It's not hard to find videos

00:29:07.436 --> 00:29:09.060
